/Q191031", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mob_rule"], "tags": ["form of government", "Political Parties & Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "ochlocracy contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Ochlocracy shaped the political dynamics of ancient democracies, illustrating how popular movements could destabilize established governance, influencing later political thought on democracy and populism.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-13T06:04:06Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27458501524", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Democracy spoiled by demagoguery and the rule of passion over reason", "newValue": "Ochlocracy, often referred to as mob rule, represents a significant deviation from democratic governance characterized by the dominance of popular emotion over rational deliberation. This concept emerged prominently during the late Classical era, particularly in the tumultuous political landscape of ancient Greece and Rome, where demagogues like Cleon and Catiline capitalized on public discontent to gain power. This manipulation of public sentiment illustrates the dangers of unchecked populism and the fragility of democratic institutions, making it a crucial subject of study in political theory and history.

